---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SocialLinks from "../components/SocialLinks.astro";
import { base } from "../lib/site";

const description =
  "Infrastructure and security engineer based in Massachusetts. Building, breaking and automating since 2016.";

const journey = [
  { year: "2016", role: "IT Support / Helpdesk", what: "Cabling, AV, break-fix, networking fundamentals" },
  { year: "2020", role: "Systems Administrator", what: "M365 migrations, HIPAA security program, MDM at scale" },
  { year: "2022", role: "IT Support and Security", what: "Vulnerability management, network config, L1 incident response" },
  { year: "2023", role: "Cloud Admin / Security", what: "XDR rollout, CASB, IAM and Conditional Access, hybrid cloud" },
  { year: "2024", role: "Infrastructure Security Engineer", what: "IaC, GitOps, DevSecOps, compliance frameworks" },
];

const certs = ["CISM", "CompTIA CCAP", "CSIS", "WGU, Network Engineering and Cybersecurity"];
const stack = [
  "Kubernetes / k3s", "FluxCD", "Azure", "Microsoft Sentinel", "Splunk / ELK",
  "Nessus / Rapid7", "Palo Alto Networks", "Zero Trust", "NIST CSF", "CIS v8",
];
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description={description}>
  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-head">
      <div class="eyebrow">About</div>
      <h1>Hey, I'm Jonathan.</h1>
      <div class="post-meta">
        <span>Boston, MA</span>
        <span>Infrastructure Security Engineer</span>
        <span>CISM</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I'm an infrastructure and security engineer based out of Massachusetts. I've been in IT since 2016.
        I started by pulling cable and troubleshooting end users at a nonprofit, worked through sysadmin and
        cloud security roles, and gradually shifted toward platform engineering, automation and security
        architecture. The through-line has always been the same: <strong>make it more reliable, more secure
        and more automated than I found it.</strong>
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm building</h2>
      <p>
        The main project is a mixed-architecture k3s cluster at home, managed entirely with FluxCD. Git is the
        only source of truth. Secrets are SOPS-encrypted, ingress goes through Cloudflare Tunnel, storage is
        Longhorn and backups run through Velero. It is where I experiment before I deploy anything at work,
        and the tooling maps directly to what production platform engineering looks like.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also keep a containerized game server, a hardened serverless API on AWS, and some security
        automation work. The full list, with source where it is public, is on the
        <a href={`${base}/projects`}>projects page</a>. Everything I learn while building goes into the
        <a href={`${base}/writing`}>build log</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Lesson learned the hard way: I once force-pushed a branch, triggered Flux pruning and wiped my
        cloudflared deployment. <code>git reflog</code> is a skill.
      </p>

      <h2>On the roadmap</h2>
      <p>
        Talos Linux, Cilium CNI, kube-vip and kube-prometheus-stack. The goal is to move from "it works" to
        "it is observable, resilient and enterprise-grade."
      </p>

      <h2>The journey</h2>
      <ol class="timeline">
        {journey.map((j) => (
          <li>
            <span class="year">{j.year}</span>
            <span class="role">{j.role}</span>
            <span class="what">{j.what}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p>The homelab is where the theory meets the metal.</p>

      <h2>Certifications and education</h2>
      <div class="chips">
        {certs.map((c) => <span class="chip">{c}</span>)}
      </div>

      <h2>Tools and stack</h2>
      <div class="chips">
        {stack.map((s) => <span class="chip">{s}</span>)}
      </div>

      <h2>Find me</h2>
      <SocialLinks />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
